- F-353 **One build can serve the compiled path with either library, shown in a scratch tree: the prelude's thirteen run-time value classes stay, and a second set under the same names, in a second class folder that the class path puts first, serves the one library.** The scratch tree is that of F-352 with its hacks, nine of them behind a selector that one environment variable sets at the compile and at the run. One JVM serves one library. There the one-line program runs against both libraries, and the `Array1` program and the dot loop against the one library, by hand and through `fortress junit`. A sample of 137 of the 876 files of `compiler_tests` shows no test that the second set disturbed; the whole compiled tracks and walk were not run. The second set's sources equal the first's under a map of eight names, since the classes differ only in the generated classes that they extend. The classes of the build that link against a prelude class by name are the thirteen, `FValue`, `MutableFValue`, `Utility`, `FException`, `FZZ32Vector` and `FStringVector`; every other mention of the prelude in the run time and the helpers is a string, 105 raise strings among them (read in the constant pools of `ProjectFortress/build`). In that build the one library's fourteen compiles took 204 s and the prelude's five 25 s, measured once on an idle machine. `main` has its own selector (F-381) and one set of run-time classes. Nothing of the scratch tree landed, and no test holds its runs. Detail: `compile-ladder/plan-31/probes/probe-y.md`, "1. What ran", "2. How the library is selected" and "5. What the second set costs".
