- F-361 **With the five number traits merged with their objects in a scratch tree, the checker count and the distance stay 0, microGPT's quick checks pass, and walk's suite differs in one `XXX` test; on `832f7d0d91`'s code the merge widens three gaps, fixed since, to the five types.** The merge makes `ZZ32`, `ZZ64`, `ZZ`, `NN64` and `RR64` value objects (F-379): 12 files, the library's text and 78 lines of walk's Java, with no checker or code-generator file and no model line. Of the 830 tests of `ant testSystem`, 829 pass, and `XXXGenericBesidePlainReturnParam` passes where a failure is expected (row 550), for a wrong reason: walk's load check loses a refusal for a generic beside a plain declaration at an object type (row 998). The other two gaps: a type parameter bounded by a value type through a trait's generic method (row 997, passed in the scratch tree by one line of walk), and the checker's refusal of a valid generic-beside-plain pair over an object domain (row 542, by reading). On that code a `typecase` arm that names a removed object, such as `Int`, matches every value with no message (row 959). On `main` the five are value objects of the one library and walk (F-379), and rows 998, 997, 542, 550 and 959 are fixed; an arm at a removed name is refused (F-382). Measured once on `832f7d0d91`, in a scratch tree that landed nothing; no test holds it; the compiled tests and `ant testFast` were not run. Detail: `compile-ladder/plan-31/probes/number-object-merge.md`, "Measurements" and "Obstacles, in the order met".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-typecase-unbound-arm/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; commit: d3747846c; note: the record's form for the gather's rewrite of 2026-11-17 21:29Z (row 959 fixed by climb batch 33's rung D): the refusal tied to the arm alone, not to the other rows

- F-363 **A superscript follows a dotted access and a parenthesised call on both paths.** `p.wq^T`, `B.n^2`, `B.f()^T` and `B.data[5]^2` parse as the operator applied to the whole dotted primary to its left, and `(f(x))^T`, `(f(x))!` and `(a^b)!` run under walk as they do compiled; `f(x)^T` and `f(x)^2` stay a static error on both paths (walk: "the argument should not be immediately followed by a non-expression element"; the checker: "Item at this location must be an expression, not an operator."). The parser reads a primary with a dot as the front of the math items that follow it (`parser/Expression.rats:386-400`), so a subscript, a parenthesised item or a tight identifier after a dotted method call parses too; of these, one test compiles a subscript, `Maker.f()[5]^2` (`compiler_tests/SubscriptThenSuperscriptCompiled`). The one node kind that the parser builds for a single item is the one the parenthesised spelling built, so neither the checker nor the code generator changed. Walk's postfix branch evaluates an operand in parentheses as one expression (`interpreter/evaluator/Evaluator.java:965-970`). So a model line may write `p.wq^T x` and `(onehot(ks, 5))^T dx`; a selector after a superscript, `B.data^T.v`, is still a syntax error, and a superscript after a subscript that is a math item of its own, `x[5]^2` or `B.f()[5]^2`, compiles (F-387) and fails under walk (row 1); in `B.data[5]^2` the subscript is part of the dotted primary. Tests: `tests/SuperscriptAfterDottedAccess.fss`, `tests/SuperscriptAfterParenthesisedCall.fss`, `tests/SuperscriptAfterCallRefused.fss` with its `.test`, and `compiler_tests/SuperscriptAfterDottedAccessCompiled.test`, `SuperscriptAfterParenthesisedCallCompiled.test`, `XXXSuperscriptAfterCallRefusedCompiled.test`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-superscript-after/REPORT.md`, "Each form on the base and after" and "The trees".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-checker-small-gaps/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: d3747846c; note: the record's form for the gather's rewrite of 2026-11-17 21:38Z (row 1005 fixed by climb batch 33's rung C) and the merged-diff review's of 22:28Z: the test's clause made its own sentence, and the compile of x[5]^2 pointed to F-387

- F-369 **A top-level tuple binding compiles: each name is a top-level variable of its element's type, and the right side is evaluated once, at the first read of any name.** The checker gives the tuple that it rebuilds from the checked elements their tuple type, from which each name's thunk reads its own (`scala_src/typechecker/impls/Decls.scala:272-277`); where only some names declare a type and the right side is no tuple expression of as many elements, it checks the right side again against each name's type (`Decls.scala:324-336`). The code generator keeps the right side's value in a hidden class `<Component>$tuple$<first name>` and gives each name its own class, whose initializer takes its element out by the tuple's typed getter (`compiler/codegen/CodeGen.java:6825`); a wildcard binds nothing. Every compiled top-level variable is initialized at its first read, not at load as under walk, so a binding of wildcards alone never evaluates its right side (row 1011). So a model line of this form compiles as written, the model's lines 17 to 19 among them, and a name bound so is typed as a single binding's is: `explorations/run-c4/src/MicroGptFlat.fss:72`, `beta1 m`, is a new checker site against the one library (F-58). Under walk the same right side runs once for each name and once more (row 1026). The checker checks a local tuple binding's declared names beside untyped names in the same way (row 1029, fixed; `compiler_tests/LocalTupleBindingMixedTypes`, `XXXLocalTupleBindingMixedRefused`), and a typed tuple binding over an `if` with tuple branches compiles (row 1031, fixed). Tests: `compiler_tests/TopLevelTupleBindingCompiled.test`, `TopLevelTupleBindingImport.test`, `TopLevelTupleBindingMixedTypes.test`, `XXXTopLevelTupleBindingType.test`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-tuple-binding/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why" and "The binding as compiled".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-checker-small-gaps/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: d3747846c; note: the record's form for the gather's rewrite of 2026-11-17 21:38Z (row 1029 fixed by climb batch 33's rung C): the checker named as the subject, the row named, the line numbers (Decls.scala:431-443) left to the rung's report, and the refusal test named by its XXX file

- F-377 **The compiled code casts `self` where the checker reads it as a listed type or as the self-type parameter.** `CodeGen.castToTypeBelow` casts a value that has self's type (the checker's `TraitSelfType` with a non-empty list: a trait whose `comprises` clause lists types, or a trait with a self-type parameter) to a declared trait type or type variable strictly below its trait. It casts at the argument of a call with one argument (a call with several arguments casts each already), at a typed binding and an assignment, at a method's returned value, at each arm of a conditional or a `case` expression, at the conditional's type, and at a function expression's and a local function's result, also where the value is a typecase whose arms each have self's type. So `den(self)` for `den(r: R)` in `trait Q comprises { R }`, `twin(self): S = self` and `mx(self, other: T): T = if lt(other, self) then self else other end` in a self-typed trait verify and run (rows 991, 695, 1050 and 1051, fixed), as the one library's `QQ comprises { Ratio }` and `StandardTotalOrder`'s `MIN`, `MAX` and `MINMAX` need. Only `self` gets the reading: the checker refuses a variable declared at the trait where a listed type is expected. A typecase with `self` in one arm and another value in the other gets no cast (row 1070), and a trait that lists a type at a `where`-clause variable gets none (row 307). An assignment from outside an object to its `var` field, of a value whose static type is a strict subtype of the field's type, fails at run time, `NoSuchMethodError`, also for `c.r := self` at a listed type (row 1053). No cast is added where the value's type is a subtype of the declared type by `extends`. Two casts are added where the verifier, which checks no interface type, needs none: at a declared trait type strictly below self's trait, a planner's default; and at an arm that is `self` where the conditional stands at a trait type, as `atQ(c: Boolean): Q = if c then self else R(0, 0) end` gains `checkcast R`, a worker's choice beyond the plan. The curator has decided neither. Tests: `compiler_tests/ComprisesValueAtListedTypeCompiled`, `ComprisesValueAtConditionalArmRun`, `ComprisesValueFromClosureRun`, `SelfTypeReturnSelfRun`, `SelfTypeReturnSelfAtTraitRun`, `SelfTypeArmRun`, `SelfTypeFromClosureRun`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-comprises-cast/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why"; `compile-ladder/rung-self-arm-closure/REPORT.md`, "1. What changed and why".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-self-arm-closure/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; from: reviews/batch-33-review.md, "2. The decisions that landed at a default"; commit: d3747846c; note: the record's form for the gather's rewrite of 2026-11-17 21:34Z (rows 1051 and 1050 fixed by climb batch 33's rung Q); the cast sites' member names (evalArg, forExprsSerial, generateActualMethodCode, forIf, forFnExpr, localFunctionClosure) left to the two reports; the open line made to cover both casts, after climb batch 33's review (Q.skeptic.1, review-queue item 571): the trait-type cast follows fork K1 of climb batch 31's record, a planner's default; the arm's cast at the conditional's own type is rung Q's, beyond fork Q1's words "the declared type"

- F-380 **A numeral at the end of a block, or in a tuple, converts where it stands on the compiled path.** At a declared type (a return type, a typed binding, a parameter, an `if`'s other branch) the compiled checker converts a numeral that ends a block after a local declaration, and each numeral element of a tuple, by the type's own coercion of the numeral itself, so the code generator folds it into a constant; such a numeral outside the type's range is refused statically, as at a block's end without a declaration. At a place that fixes no type, a numeral that ends a block after a local declaration or a local function takes a numeral's default type, and so does each numeral element of a tuple there (row 1060, fixed). At such a place, a `try` that ends in numerals keeps the numeral's own type and is refused (row 1074), and so is a `case` or a `try` whose branches are numeral tuples at an untyped tuple binding (row 1075). Tests: `compiler_tests/NumeralEndsBlockAfterDeclarationCompiled`, `BlockEndsInTupleOfNumeralCompiled`, `TupleBindingIfBranchesCompiled`, `NumeralJoinBeyondRange.fss` with `XXXNumeralJoinBeyondRange.test`; `NumeralEndsUntypedBlockCompiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-numeral-block-end/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why", which names the checker's members; `compile-ladder/rung-numeral-block-end/SKEPTIC.md`, "Findings and fixes".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-checker-small-gaps/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: d3747846c; note: the record's form for the gather's rewrite of 2026-11-17 21:38Z (row 1060 fixed by climb batch 33's rung C): "takes its default" made plain, the row named, Functionals.scala:1290-1294 and :1332-1333 left to the rung's report

- F-381 **The compile command reads the one library where `FORTRESS_LIBRARY=one` (or `-Dfortress.library=one`) selects it, keeps its caches in `default_repository/caches-one`, and its tests run in `testFast`'s onelibrary track.** The selection is read once per JVM (`ProjectProperties.compiledPathUsesOneLibrary`); `Shell.useCompilerLibraries()` follows it, and the two class path scripts follow the variable only. Unset, every command reads the prelude as before. A program compiled under the selection does not run yet, since the one library has no jars (F-34; row 1062). Tests: `ProjectFortress/one_library_tests/OneLibraryHalf.test`, `OneLibraryPrintBig.test` and `XXXOneLibraryRunHalf.test`, run by `ant testOneLibrary` and by `ant testFast`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-library-selector/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why", "The tests: the failing run on the base, the passing run" and "Wrong pairings, and the run under the selection".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-library-selector/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; from: compile-ladder/rung-library-selector/REPORT.md, "Wrong pairings, and the run under the selection"; commit: d3747846c; note: cut to the record's form from the gather's draft of 2026-11-17 21:27Z and the merged-diff review's rewrite of 22:28Z: WellKnownNames' copy of the flag and the two tests' failing messages on the base ('QQ is undefined.', 'Variable big is not defined.') left to the rung's report

- F-382 **A `typecase` arm whose name names no type is a static error on both paths, 'X is undefined.'; an arm at a type parameter in scope tests against its static argument, and only an element of a tuple of names still binds.** The disambiguator, which both paths run, resolves a clause's type as it resolves every other type: a bare arm `Id =>`, or a name in an arrow type or a static argument inside the arm, that names no type variable in scope and no declared type is a static error at the name (`compiler/disambiguator/TypeDisambiguator.java`). So a binder over every value is written `x: Any =>`. An element of a tuple of names, as `(x, y) =>`, is the team's binding form and binds, so under walk an undefined name there takes a value of every type (row 1064). Tests: `tests/TypecaseUndefinedArmWalk.fss`, `TypecaseUndefinedNameInArmTypeWalk.fss`, `TypecaseTypeParamArmWalk.fss`; `compiler_tests/XXXTypecaseUndefinedArmCompiled`, `XXXTypecaseUndefinedNameInArmTypeCompiled`, `TypecaseTypeParamArmCompiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-typecase-unbound-arm/REPORT.md`, "1. What changed and why".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-typecase-unbound-arm/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; commit: d3747846c; note: cut to the record's form from the gather's draft of 2026-11-17 21:29Z: "refused when names are bound" made "a static error"; the members forTypecaseClause and handleTypeName and the compiled tests' names left to the rung's report; row 1064's claim given in a few words. The tuple form is the landed default of fork D1 of climb batch 33; the review's finding 1 puts it to the curator

- F-383 **Walk's harness counts a static error that a phase raises before the run, such as the disambiguator's, as a refusal at load, so a walk `.test` whose `load_` keys expect a refusal at load passes on such an error that its keys match.** `ProjectFortress/src/com/sun/fortress/tests/unit_tests/FileTests.java`, `InterpreterTest.thrownAtLoad`. Test: `tests/TypecaseUndefinedArmWalk.test`. A compiled refusal test is still `XXX`-named: a plain-named compile test is red whenever the compile reports errors. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-typecase-unbound-arm/REPORT.md`, "10. Decisions".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-typecase-unbound-arm/REPORT.md, "10. Decisions"; commit: d3747846c; note: the record's form for the gather's draft of 2026-11-17 21:29Z: the title's "names a refusal of the disambiguator as one of walk's load check" said plainly; FileTests.java:869-878 read at d3747846c

- F-384 **The code generator's kind environment holds the static parameters in scope where it reads a declaration's types, and a `typecase` clause's tuple-pattern names are bound on the compiled path.** A type's inherited methods are read with the type's static parameters and `where` clause, a generic function's or method's body is compiled with its own, and `forTypecase` binds each name of a clause's tuple pattern to its element (`compiler/codegen/CodeGen.java`). So a generic trait's member under a `where`-clause `comprises`, a call of it in a generic body, and a `typecase` of the one library's `app` form whose arms call no method on a name typed by an intersection compile and run (rows 812, 822 and 1066). A method call on a name typed by an intersection compiles by F-386's cast; no test calls one on a tuple pattern's name, the form of `app`'s arms 2 and 3. F-34 gives the code generator's measure on the one library since this repair. A clause's name bound at a trait above its selector's object type fails verification at a method call on it (row 1067). Tests: `compiler_tests/ComprisesWhereMemberCompiled.test`, `TypecaseGenericArmsCompiled.test`, `TypecaseTupleNamesCompiled.test`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-kind-env-generic/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why" and "The one library's four declarations".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-kind-env-generic/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; from: compile-ladder/rung-kind-env-generic/REPORT.md, "The one library's four declarations"; commit: d3747846c; note: cut to the record's form from the gather's draft of 2026-11-17 21:31Z and the merged-diff review's rewrite of 22:28Z: CodeGen.java:498-502, :2749-2756 and :2578-2610 left to the rung's report; rung V's own measure (15 refused in 5 kinds on 18d5b51578) moved to F-34, which owns the count

- F-385 **The class loader chooses a compiled call's opcode from the owner's class file, without loading the owner.** Where an instantiated template calls a method by `invokeinterface` on a type that becomes a plain class name, `MethodInstantiater.visitMethodInsn` reads `ACC_INTERFACE` from the class file that the loader would define for that name, and uses `invokevirtual` for a class (`runtimeSystem/MethodInstantiater.java`). So an object that lists a self-typed trait first, whose JVM superclass is that trait's default methods at the object, runs a default body that calls a method on a value of the self-type parameter (row 1052, fixed). Two limits, by reading, neither measured nor held by a test. The read keeps no cache, unlike the loader's lookup of generic expansions beside it, so each such call site of each expansion reads a class file. A class that the loader generates, such as a generic object's, has no class file, so the loader loads it as before, and a generic object that lists a self-typed trait first may still fail as row 1052 did. Test: `compiler_tests/SelfTypeParameterReceiverRun`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-self-arm-closure/REPORT.md`, "1. What changed and why", item 3; `reviews/batch-33-review.md`, "2. The decisions that landed at a default".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-self-arm-closure/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; from: reviews/batch-33-review.md, "2. The decisions that landed at a default"; commit: d3747846c; note: cut to the record's form from the gather's draft of 2026-11-17 21:34Z, its line numbers (:268-272, :304) left to the rung's report; the two limits from climb batch 33's review (section 2, Q2, and "Corrections owed in the record"): the team's lookup beside it caches (xlationForGeneric, stemToXlation)

- F-386 **A dotted call on a value whose static type is an intersection in which no conjunct lies below the others compiles as a call on the conjunct that has the declaration the checker chose.** Such a type comes from a call typed by the intersection of its candidates' return types (P-60) and from a `typecase` binder whose arm's type neither lies below nor above the selector's. The code generator casts the receiver to that conjunct, the most specific where several qualify, else the first in the intersection's order; the value's own method runs, as under walk. Where the conjuncts are two instances of one generic trait, as `List[\E\] ∩ List[\T\]` in the one library's `app`, the choice changes no answer, by reading: no type extends two instances of one generic trait (P-43, the exclusion rule), so such a value exists only where `E` and `T` are one type; no test calls such a method. A getter goes to a conjunct whose method of that name takes no argument; a method overloaded across the conjuncts and a generic method compile too. On the one library this emits `__filter`. The checker refuses a method of a type parameter's bound on such a value where a conjunct is the parameter (row 1072). Tests: `compiler_tests/CoverageReturnMethodCall.test`, `compiler_tests/TypecaseBinderMethodCall.test`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-intersection-receiver/REPORT.md`, "1. What changed and why" and "5. Programs run old against new".
  from: compile-ladder/rung-intersection-receiver/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; from: reviews/batch-33-review.md, "2. The decisions that landed at a default"; commit: d3747846c; note: cut to the record's form from the gather's draft of 2026-11-17 21:36Z: the title's "none of its conjuncts lies below" made plain; "whose call has no arrow" and Library/FortressLibrary.fss:1489 left to the rung's report; the reading on two instances of one generic trait from climb batch 33's review (section 2, V.worker.2; review-routed.1 owes its test, PLAN.md:889)

- F-387 **The compiled path compiles a primary whose math items all apply in turn to its front: `x[5]^2` is `(x[5])^2` and `g[1][2]` is `(g[1])[2]`; and a subscript as a math item before a tightly juxtaposed item: `x[5]k` is `(x[5]) k`.** The checker checks the one application that the reassociation leaves, at the expected type, and writes each subscript as a call of the subscript operator's method, as the desugaring before it writes every other subscript (`scala_src/typechecker/impls/Operators.scala`). Walk still fails on these forms (row 1), so a program for both paths writes `(x[5])^2`. Test: `compiler_tests/SubscriptThenSuperscriptCompiled.test`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-checker-small-gaps/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why", item 2.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-checker-small-gaps/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: d3747846c; note: cut to the record's form from the gather's draft of 2026-11-17 21:38Z (row 1005 fixed by climb batch 33's rung C): Operators.scala:313-321 and :360-362 left to the rung's report

- P-12 **After the switch-over, walk runs the compiled type checker on its programs, and then uses the static coercions that the checker inserts.** The curator wants the checker usable on the programs that walk runs. Today `fortress typecheck` checks a program against the compiler's prelude unless `FORTRESS_LIBRARY=one` selects the one library, so by default it is no check of walk's programs (F-9 holds this fact). Walk is not changed for this before the switch-over. After it, walk runs the checker on its programs and so gains their static types; F-9 and F-290 say what walk and the library lack for that step. Walk then uses the static coercions that the checker inserts, in place of its choice on the run-time value (P-59, walk's choice of coercions until the switch-over).
  from: compile-ladder/rung-library-selector/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: d3747846c; note: the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 (HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-10-31)"): a sentence that the tree made false, changed by the fewest words; under FORTRESS_LIBRARY=one, typecheck reads the one library (F-9; F-381; Shell.java:377-388; row 69's note); what he decided, where it applies and what it requires are unchanged
